# Assignment 3: Access Control, Password Security, and SSH Log Analysis

## Overview

In this assignment, I examined access-control models, password security, attribute-based authorization, and authentication-log analysis. I compared Discretionary Access Control (DAC), Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), and Mandatory Access Control (MAC), with a focus on how RBAC reduces the complexity of managing permissions by assigning permissions to roles rather than directly to every individual user.

I analyzed password salting and legacy LM hashing. I explained how unique salts reduce the effectiveness of rainbow tables and large-scale password cracking, and I evaluated weaknesses in the LM hash design, including case insensitivity, password splitting, lack of salting, use of DES, null padding, and the absence of a configurable work factor.

I designed an access-control model using age, membership type, movie rating, and release date. I translated the attribute-based requirements into roles, permissions, and an access matrix while accounting for real-world changes such as user registration, birthdays, subscription updates, new content, and account deletion.

To complete the assignment, I used Python, Pandas, regular expressions, and Matplotlib to analyze OpenSSH authentication logs. I identified source IP addresses associated with repeated invalid-user login attempts, filtered events to reduce duplicate counting, and examined the timing of failed authentication activity. My analysis identified a morning concentration of suspicious login attempts, with the highest observed activity at 9:00 AM.

